<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince Shōtoku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">574–622</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Shōtoku Taishi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Shōtoku (574–622) was a regent and statesman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamato-court">Yamato court</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> under Empress Suiko. Tradition credits him with the Seventeen-Article Constitution of 604, the adoption of Chinese-style court ranks, embassies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the founding of temples such as Hōryū-ji. A great patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he became a revered figure in Japanese religion, though historians debate how much of his record is legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-shotoku.jpg" alt="Prince Shōtoku" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sh%C5%8Dtoku_Taishi_Sh%C5%8Dmanky%C5%8D_K%C5%8Dsan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
